concepts.content:
Homogeneous coordinates allow using transformations as matrix multiplications. We can build complex transformations by combining these four transformations:

$$\mathbf{p}' = 
    \begin{bmatrix}
    1 & q_x & 0 \\
    q_y & 1 & 0 \\
    0 & 0 & 1
    \end{bmatrix}
    \begin{bmatrix}
    s_x & 0 & 0 \\
    0 & s_y & 0 \\
    0 & 0 & 1
    \end{bmatrix}
    \begin{bmatrix}
    \cos(\theta) & \sin(\theta) & 0 \\
    -\sin(\theta) & \cos(\theta) & 0 \\
    0 & 0 & 1
    \end{bmatrix}
    \begin{bmatrix}
    1 & 0 & t_x \\
    0 & 1 & t_y \\
    0 & 0 & 1
    \end{bmatrix}
    \mathbf{p}$$

(see figure: Summary of 2D geometric transformations and their formulation in homogeneous coordinates.)

As matrix multiplication is noncommutative, the order in which operations are performed is important. All of the geometric transformations described are relative to the origin. To rotate an image around an arbitrary central location, you must first translate to put that location at the origin, then rotate, and then translate back:

$$\mathbf{p}' = 
    \begin{bmatrix}
    1 & 0 & t_x \\
    0 & 1 & t_y \\
    0 & 0 & 1
    \end{bmatrix}
    \begin{bmatrix}
    \cos(\theta) & \sin(\theta) & 0 \\
    -\sin(\theta) & \cos(\theta) & 0 \\
    0 & 0 & 1
    \end{bmatrix}
    \begin{bmatrix}
    1 & 0 & -t_x \\
    0 & 1 & -t_y \\
    0 & 0 & 1
    \end{bmatrix}
    \mathbf{p}$$

